1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.289
Hello, I'm Armando Fox from UC Berkeley and today I have the pleasure of telling you about

2
00:00:06.289 --> 00:00:12.418
our experience bootstrapping a computer-based testing facility using shared lab space.

3
00:00:12.419 --> 00:00:18.583
I encourage you to read the paper if you are not familiar with the benefits of computer-based testing, but by computer-based

4
00:00:18.583 --> 00:00:24.788
testing facility, I'm talking about a facility for administering computer-based exams, whether they're manually

5
00:00:24.788 --> 00:00:30.799
graded or machine-graded, and a few of the important reasons to do this, which we discuss in the paper and I will

6
00:00:30.799 --> 00:00:36.926
not discuss further in this talk. The Proctors in our system are non-academic student workers, as opposed

7
00:00:36.927 --> 00:00:42.977
to instructors or teaching assistants. The facility is centrally managed with locked-down computers that

8
00:00:42.977 --> 00:00:49.144
are connected permanently to our wired network. The exams are all fixed length, a uniform length of one hour

9
00:00:49.144 --> 00:00:55.863
during the semester, or two hours during finals week, and taken over a two or typically three-day window,

10
00:00:55.864 --> 00:01:01.931
and we use PrairieLearn as the software for administering and authoring the exams. All of these

11
00:01:01.931 --> 00:01:07.954
choices and important reasons why we chose them are justified in the paper. I'd like to

12
00:01:07.954 --> 00:01:14.570
give you two major takeaways from our work. The first is: probably no single unit at your institution

13
00:01:14.571 --> 00:01:20.642
owns exam administration. Exam administration involves physical space, it involves proctors,

14
00:01:20.642 --> 00:01:26.780
it involves software if computers are involved, it involves accommodating students with disabilities. All of these

15
00:01:26.781 --> 00:01:32.967
are usually distributed across different departments. So, to stand up a computer-based testing facility you will need

16
00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:39.391
to coordinate across stakeholder boundaries and across units that normally do not collaborate closely with each

17
00:01:39.392 --> 00:01:46.877
other. And at the same time you'll have to tailor your messaging to each of those different stakeholders in order to move your agenda forward.

18
00:01:46.877 --> 00:01:52.893
The second takeaway is: even though I'm going to describe how we scaled up to a much larger facility, you

19
00:01:52.893 --> 00:01:59.332
don't have to do that to get most of the benefits. This is not an all or nothing  endeavor. It's an all or something endeavor.

20
00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:05.467
A lot of the benefits appear at smaller scale and the devil is in the details, which is why I hope that this talk will encourage

21
00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:11.516
you to read the paper to get those details. Let me briefly give you an overview of where we

22
00:02:11.516 --> 00:02:18.031
started and where we got to. For the first time in fall of 2023 we decided to try computer-based

23
00:02:18.032 --> 00:02:24.264
exams and we reserved partial time in two shared lab facilities that you can see here. They're unremarkable

24
00:02:24.264 --> 00:02:30.835
computer labs with wired desktop computers at each workstation. And initially we began with two labs having

25
00:02:30.836 --> 00:02:37.227
a total of about 55 seats between them; a single course run by me, with an enrollment of a little over 200

26
00:02:37.227 --> 00:02:43.480
students; we only used the facility for the final exam, which at that time was two and a half hours.

27
00:02:43.481 --> 00:02:50.247
That accounts for about 700 student exam hours given that a few students need extra time due to a disability.

28
00:02:50.248 --> 00:02:56.276
The exam was proctored by the course staff from my own course and the entire process was overseen by

29
00:02:56.277 --> 00:03:02.372
my co-instructor and me. And the operations were funded by a research grant on mastery

30
00:03:02.372 --> 00:03:08.531
learning. So this really began as a pilot, funded as a research project and operated within the scope of one small

31
00:03:08.531 --> 00:03:14.611
course. Three years later, in Fall 2026, you can see a photo of our newly opened

32
00:03:14.612 --> 00:03:20.697
100-seat permanent computer-based testing facility which is open 40 hours a week, and we're aiming to open it more

33
00:03:20.697 --> 00:03:26.763
in the spring semester. We're currently serving 24 courses and over 10,000 unique Berkeley

34
00:03:26.764 --> 00:03:33.026
undergraduates have gone through the facility at this point in this semester. That's more than a quarter of our total undergraduate

35
00:03:33.026 --> 00:03:39.306
population. Over 100 exams are scheduled, accounting for about 40,000 seated

36
00:03:39.306 --> 00:03:45.688
student-exam-hours. The proctors are non-academic hourly student workers. They are not teaching assistants

37
00:03:45.688 --> 00:03:52.170
or instructors, and the paper describes why this is an important choice. We now have a full-time

38
00:03:52.171 --> 00:03:58.591
career employee who is managing this facility and we'll soon have additional staff there. And the entire service

39
00:03:58.591 --> 00:04:05.551
is funded by the central IT unit of campus -- our information technology and instructional technology unit.

40
00:04:05.552 --> 00:04:11.653
So, how did we achieve that scale-up? One thing that we had to figure out is

41
00:04:11.653 --> 00:04:17.698
a chicken and egg problem, as we call it. On the one hand, we wanted to recruit instructors because

42
00:04:17.699 --> 00:04:23.929
we needed to demonstrate to campus that with a successful pilot, there would be demand to expand and centrally

43
00:04:23.929 --> 00:04:29.943
fund this facility. So, our message to instructors was: Let's show demand to the institution that will

44
00:04:29.943 --> 00:04:36.284
get us an institutional commitment. Of course, as with any new technology, there are instructors

45
00:04:36.284 --> 00:04:42.620
who were eager to adopt it early, there were instructors who were extremely skeptical, and there's a large middle ground.

46
00:04:42.621 --> 00:04:48.678
Obviously, we went after the early adopters first, to try to build our support base. We started

47
00:04:48.678 --> 00:04:54.806
incrementally by migrating homeworks and low-stakes quizzes before doing real exams, and we helped instructors

48
00:04:54.807 --> 00:05:01.361
with this process by handholding them quite a bit. One lesson we learned in that is that the value proposition

49
00:05:01.361 --> 00:05:07.470
to instructors for migrating to this format is much more obvious than it is for teaching assistants. Most teaching

50
00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:13.538
assistants do not stay with the course for longer than one or two semesters, whereas most instructors have to steward it into

51
00:05:13.539 --> 00:05:20.013
the future. Now, at the same time as we were recruiting instructors, we were also lobbying the institution.

52
00:05:20.013 --> 00:05:26.543
And our message to the institution was: Unless there's a commitment of institutional support, it's going to be difficult to get instructors

53
00:05:26.543 --> 00:05:33.055
to sign on. They won't want to invest time in creating new content that might not be useful in a couple of years.

54
00:05:33.056 --> 00:05:39.460
So, our lesson there was that there are multiple stakeholders involved in making this happen—I'll talk about a few of them in a moment—

55
00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:45.660
and every opportunity to message a small success to one or multiple of those stakeholders, in terms that they can understand,

56
00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:49.499
is one more way that you can advance this agenda.

57
00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:56.013
We can divide our experience into two periods: the bootstrap phase and the growth phase.

58
00:05:56.013 --> 00:06:02.026
We started during the pandemic in 2020 and were able to secure internal "micro-grants" from the university

59
00:06:02.027 --> 00:06:08.907
to develop assessments for mastery learning. Those would be the exact type of assessments needed to administer computer-based

60
00:06:08.908 --> 00:06:15.031
exams, as our paper describes. The success in doing that and deploying it in a couple of courses allowed us to

61
00:06:15.031 --> 00:06:21.619
get a much larger grant from the California Education Learning Lab, which allowed us to start configuring our own local servers

62
00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:27.913
around the open source PrairieLearn software, while students were still stuck at home but could take quizzes through

63
00:06:27.913 --> 00:06:34.362
PrairieLearn proctored by webcam. We began a search for physical space to move those assessments to,

64
00:06:34.362 --> 00:06:40.595
once the lockdown had ended, and we were in the meantime able to get a scale-up grant by demonstrating that

65
00:06:40.596 --> 00:06:46.601
we could scale up our success in small courses to much larger courses and many more students in departments

66
00:06:46.601 --> 00:06:52.823
if we had more resources. And finally, we located the space, these shared labs that I showed

67
00:06:52.824 --> 00:06:58.499
you earlier, where at least one course could pilot doing a single exam in the CBTF format. 

68
00:06:58.500 --> 00:06:58.704
a 30 space. A 30,

69
00:06:58.704 --> 00:07:04.841
the success of that phase began a growth phase in spring 2024 when we were still using a shared lab

70
00:07:04.841 --> 00:07:10.907
facility and hosting our own PrairieLearn servers to getting a dedicated 30-seat lab to

71
00:07:10.907 --> 00:07:17.416
use as a computer-based testing facility. The decision was then made that we were going to transfer dev/ops

72
00:07:17.417 --> 00:07:23.802
to PrairieLearn.com and pay for hosted service rather than continuing to host it ourselves. The paper explains

73
00:07:23.802 --> 00:07:29.999
this choice in more detail. In Fall 2025, we were miraculously able to find a large

74
00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.410
space that we would be able to take over to build the 100-seat facility that opened in Fall 2026,

75
00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:39.066
just a few weeks ago as this talk is being recorded. 

76
00:07:39.066 --> 00:07:41.406
I should point out that as we were growing

77
00:07:41.407 --> 00:07:48.243
the number of total student-exam-hours, we were also growing the number of distinct departments and courses that were using it.

78
00:07:48.244 --> 00:07:54.284
A wide variety of courses from departments all over campus was further evidence to the administration that this was a

79
00:07:54.284 --> 00:07:57.033
general public good that was worth supporting.

80
00:07:57.033 --> 00:08:03.589
During the entire growth period, we constantly looked for opportunities to message our different constituencies

81
00:08:03.589 --> 00:08:10.005
about the transformative benefits of computer-based testing. For example, for our Student Accommodations Office,

82
00:08:10.006 --> 00:08:16.447
which handles accommodations for students who need extra time on exams, reduced distraction, and so forth,

83
00:08:16.448 --> 00:08:23.535
we could take a lot of work off of their plate, and at the same time give more flexibility to the students that they wish to serve.

84
00:08:23.536 --> 00:08:29.583
Instructors who are concerned about students using AI or other unauthorized resources in an unproctored

85
00:08:29.583 --> 00:08:35.741
environment were reassured by the high academic integrity of the computer-based testing facility. And for their

86
00:08:35.741 --> 00:08:42.184
TAs and their instructional staff, quality of life was greatly increased by not having to worry about the administration

87
00:08:42.184 --> 00:08:48.272
of exams, the proctoring of exams, and all of the things that go along with that, allowing those TA's

88
00:08:48.272 --> 00:08:54.470
more time to do the high-value job of teaching and working directly with students. For those of us who care

89
00:08:54.471 --> 00:09:00.655
about equity in education, Mastery Learning, which was the research project from which all of this originally

90
00:09:00.656 --> 00:09:07.410
sprang, is known to be a way to reduce equity gaps that result from uneven prior preparation; and computer-based

91
00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:13.727
testing is a key ingredient that enables doing so. From the registrar's point of view, fewer

92
00:09:13.727 --> 00:09:19.948
classrooms have to be occupied just for exam proctoring, and from the administration's point of view,

93
00:09:19.949 --> 00:09:26.066
not only is there a cost reduction from centralizing the resources required to administer an

94
00:09:26.066 --> 00:09:32.176
exam, but also from not having to rent out some of the large campus spaces such as gymnasia for final

95
00:09:32.176 --> 00:09:34.633
exams during final exam week.

96
00:09:34.633 --> 00:09:41.034
Let me next talk about some highlights of lessons we learned doing all of this. The biggest

97
00:09:41.034 --> 00:09:47.188
one is probably to think big from the first time you start, even if you never get much larger.

98
00:09:47.188 --> 00:09:54.212
What happens, for example, if students wait too long to schedule their exams and can't find a slot, or if they no-show for their reservations?

99
00:09:54.212 --> 00:10:00.595
You have to have a policy that when scaled up, would not result in an unrealistic amount of work for whoever is staffing

100
00:10:00.596 --> 00:10:06.843
and managing the CBTF. What if a faculty member modifies their exam at the last minute and causes it to break

101
00:10:06.843 --> 00:10:13.124
or have bugs in it? There needs to be a protocol so that faculty realize they can't just restart an exam

102
00:10:13.125 --> 00:10:19.247
or reschedule it at will, because the CBTF might be otherwise busy. Similarly, if there are

103
00:10:19.247 --> 00:10:25.347
bugs in the exam or ambiguous questions, the faculty member must realize that it will be their responsibility to deal with

104
00:10:25.347 --> 00:10:31.445
all of this after the exam is closed. So the first lesson from all of this is: a lot of the benefits of

105
00:10:31.446 --> 00:10:37.532
computer-based testing facilities apply even at small scale, but it's essential that you do not

106
00:10:37.532 --> 00:10:43.599
allow small scale to relax the expectations you set. The policies and procedures that you set at small scale are

107
00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:46.499
very difficult to change later when you scale up.

108
00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:52.508
Now, that lesson may not seem terribly surprising, but we did learn a few that genuinely

109
00:10:52.508 --> 00:10:58.564
were. I'll give you a few highlights here, but again, please read the paper because there's not enough time in the talk

110
00:10:58.565 --> 00:11:04.698
to go into these in any detail. There's many categories of lessons facing your different stakeholders. For

111
00:11:04.699 --> 00:11:10.863
example, in terms of physical space, contrary to our expectations, a larger challenge than finding funding was

112
00:11:10.863 --> 00:11:17.328
finding actual space to put a permanent CBTF in. This may or may not be a problem at your institution.

113
00:11:17.329 --> 00:11:23.411
In terms of IT and networking, even though the software is open source and freely downloadable, we don't

114
00:11:23.412 --> 00:11:29.594
recommend self-hosting. The paper explains how we reached this decision. In terms of accessibility, we learned many

115
00:11:29.594 --> 00:11:35.698
valuable lessons, including the fact that every institution has a different workflow for how students with exam

116
00:11:35.698 --> 00:11:41.753
accommodations are handled. Also, for most student disability offices, the model of proctoring an

117
00:11:41.753 --> 00:11:47.773
exam for the entire class is very different from what they're accustomed to. So, work early and often with your

118
00:11:47.774 --> 00:11:54.315
disabled students' office, to make sure that you're working in sync. At the same time, for students

119
00:11:54.315 --> 00:12:00.750
as well as faculty, this is a brand new way of doing exams, and you will need to realize that every expectation

120
00:12:00.751 --> 00:12:07.464
and process is going to be different for them. Faculty cannot tweak exams until the last minute. Students must self-schedule

121
00:12:07.464 --> 00:12:13.478
when they take the exam, and they shouldn't wait too long. And as they say in marketing, you'll have to repeat these messages seven times

122
00:12:13.478 --> 00:12:20.065
before they're really heard and retained. And finally, literally walk through every procedure.

123
00:12:20.066 --> 00:12:26.598
Can the students get to the testing center? Does the building require special access after hours? Make sure every stakeholder's

124
00:12:26.599 --> 00:12:31.966
experience has actually been dry-run to avoid some of the "gotchas" that we describe in the paper.

125
00:12:31.966 --> 00:12:38.115
I'll close with a few of the thoughts on the two takeaways that I framed initially.

126
00:12:38.116 --> 00:12:44.140
First of all, as I said, no single unit at your institution probably owns everything associated with exam

127
00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:50.821
administration. You will be herding cats across boundaries, but the results are well worth it.

128
00:12:50.822 --> 00:12:57.231
Second, it's not all or nothing, it's all or something. You can do a smaller part-time CBTF,

129
00:12:57.231 --> 00:13:03.309
you don't have to do it at scale, and you will still realize most of the benefits. Just make sure that when you are planning your

130
00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:09.458
policies and procedures, you plan them to scale up. And as you're scaling up, message every victory in

131
00:13:09.458 --> 00:13:15.845
terms that each of your stakeholders can understand. They will see that there are some benefits to be had at small scale

132
00:13:15.846 --> 00:13:21.951
and they can be transformative at large scale. If we did it at my bureaucratic institution, we're confident that

133
00:13:21.951 --> 00:13:28.236
you can do it as well. I look forward to hearing from you, and if there's any way that I can help or provide additional information

134
00:13:28.236 --> 00:13:35.133
beyond what's in the paper or in this talk that would help you set this up at your own institution, I'll be more than happy to do so.

135
00:13:35.133 --> 00:13:40.232
Thank you very much for your time and attention, and GO BEARS!

